**Why not a literal callout inside the trigger:** Apex forbids HTTP callouts in an `after insert` trigger ("uncommitted work pending"), so the trigger must hand off to an async execution that runs immediately after the insert commits. I use Queueables enqueued directly by the trigger (not `@future`, which is capped at 50 calls per transaction — too few for 200-account batches). Because a single transaction may only make 100 callouts, the trigger enqueues one Queueable per chunk of up to 100 accounts (a 200-account marketplace batch → 2 jobs, 200 requests). Crucially, jobs are **never chained** from inside `execute()` — chained Queueables re-enter synchronously in test context with already-exhausted governor limits, which caused the previous "Maximum stack depth has been reached" failure. Every job is enqueued up front from the trigger, so each runs in its own transaction with a full limit set. Each account still gets exactly one ERP request.

